        "id": 1291841
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-16T11:06:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should work on the latency_sensitive concept, firsts of all because it\u0027s a range value and thus better than a flag from the \"resource management\" story standpoint.\n\nMoreover, being a more abstract concept, we have more freedom in mapping it in userspace.\n\nIf a task is latency-sensitive we want to run AND complete it sooner, relatively to others. Thus, we wanna run it:\n\n  - (possibly) on an idle CPU             \u003d\u003e to start it sooner\n  - on a relatively high frequency        \u003d\u003e to complete it sooner\n  AND\n  - with reduced chances to be preempted  \u003d\u003e to complete it sooner\n\nIMO we already have all the ingredients to cook such a recipe, considering all the limitations of the FAIR class of course.\n\nIn principle we just need to consider prefer_idle a task which matches these TWO conditions:\n\n a) cpu.uclamp_min \u003e 0  (default value)\n b) cpu.shares \u003e 1024   (default value)\n\nIn this case, we actually already have all that we need in mainline and we can also distinguish between:\n\n - boosted tasks,     where only a) applies\n - prefer_idle tasks, where both a) and b) must apply\n\nIf we really need also the \"prefer_idle NON boosted\" case, then maybe we can get it by just adding in a threshold in the condition a) above.\n\nThoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "6eeeb960_dfadba1d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 14,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 15,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "f667511c769e4814e45319553946371ec3327178",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
